SM(497,{"n":"Jude 1-11","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Jude 1-11.mp3","d":2548.9,"s":[[0.32,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.96,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in biblical studies, and it's an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[20.88,"We'll go ahead and get started."],[23.52,"And tonight, as I mentioned, we're going to be going through the Epistle of Saint Jude."],[30.0,"Saint Jude is short, and so there are no chapter numbers."],[36.48,"Just verses. It's all one chapter."],[38.56,"In the Greek, as I mentioned, it's sort of a longish paragraph."],[47.12,"But the reason to reiterate from back when we introduced 1st and 2nd Peter and Jude, the reason we're doing Jude out of order, in between first and second Peter instead of waiting until after we do first, second, and third John, is that as we mentioned, then pretty much the entire text of the Epistle of Saint Jude is incorporated into 2 Peter chapter 2, um, which is one of the weird things in the New Testament."],[81.08,"Um, but so based on that, that St."],[85.34,"Jude's epistle clearly was written first, as we talked about, I'm not gonna go through all that again, but was written first and then was incorporated in that way into the text of uh 2 Peter."],[98.38,"We're gonna talk about it first, and then when we next get together, which is gonna be a little while. but for people who say this recording will just be next week"],[108.1,"But um be a little when we next get together, we're gonna start on 2 Peter, and we'll talk more about that, especially when we get to chapter 2."],[118.34,"Um, so as we mentioned, uh St."],[123.38,"Jude's Epistle identifies itself as we're gonna see in the first verse is being written by uh Jude, the brother of James, which is about, as we were just saying, the vaguest sort of intro you could give for Palestinian Judaism, because that means Judah, the brother of Jacob, which there were probably like a thousand of those."],[152.52,"In any given place."],[153.96,"But traditionally, this uh Judah the brother of Jacob has been identified as one of the brothers of the Lord."],[163.29,"One of the sons, because we're told when their names are listed, there is one named Judas or Jude."],[171.95,"Judas being the Greek form of Judah."],[174.35,"Um as we talked about in our intro to St."],[179.15,"James Epistle, St."],[180.75,"James, the brother of the Lord is obviously this major figure in the early church."],[185.15,"As we also mentioned before in the introduction to this set of books, um people sort of spasmodic.ally doubt that that Saint Jude wrote this"],[197.75,"I say spasmodically because there's not a real reason to."],[202.49,"So there's no, there are no like apocryphal texts attributed to this Saint Jude."],[209.66,"Where someone would be like, oh, Saint Jude, he's famous in the church."],[213.5,"We need to say this was written by him, even though it wasn't."],[218.27,"Even if for some reason, maybe, maybe you could hypothesize there were a raft of pseude epigraphic, apocryphal, Saint Jude texts in the ancient church, and we just lost all of them."],[231.52,"Even if that were the case, again, the introduction does not identify. him as the brother of the Lord"],[238.9,"So if you're trying to fake being written by somebody famous, you do it less casually than that."],[248.4,"If your goal is, you know, I want to give my text authority by claiming it was written by Abe Lincoln."],[257.09,"Then I'm not going to start it by saying, you know, uh a book by Abraham from Illinois."],[268.79,"Like, if I'm trying to try to do a fake Abraham Lincoln text, I'm gonna say, you know, a book by Abraham Lincoln, 16th president of the United States."],[280.12,"On and on and on and on and on."],[281.58,"To try and make that claim."],[282.78,"And that's what you see when you look at actual apocryphal pseude epigraphic meaning written by a false, uh falsely named author."],[292.62,"When you look at those texts, they all do that."],[295.82,"You get this paragraph at the beginning, sort of laying out, you know, like the apocryphal St."],[301.58,"James literature, which would be the closest thing we have to this, that claims claims to be written by St."],[306.7,"James, the brother of the Lord."],[308.14,"It's St. James, the brother of the Lord, first bishop of Jerusalem, who was initiated into the secret mysteries of faith by Christ Hsimelf after his resurrection, on and on and on and on and on."],[320.59,"Um, to try and make that claim."],[322.43,"So there's no reason to doubt that this person wrote it because this person isn't famous otherwise"],[329.75,"There's nothing else that claims to be written by him."],[332.79,"So it would be weird to make that identification falsely."],[337.91,"Um it seems to me simplest to just say, yeah, that's probably who wrote it."],[345.24,"And otherwise, uh, he was he was uh unremarkable, at least in terms of leading leaving behind other other literature."],[354.12,"Uh, as we also mentioned, because of the connections to 2 Peter, uh, this is probably uh most likely the most plausible explanation is that this is coming out of uh the Christian community in Rome, meaning the city of Rome itself, because that's clearly where 2 Peter is coming from."],[377.84,"Uh and this gets incorporated into it."],[381.2,"And as we talked about, and we'll talk about more next time, uh, 2 Peter is is sort of incorporating traditional Roman teaching and preaching from St."],[391.2,"Peter and and others uh into its text."],[395.28,"So um that's sort of the background of this."],[399.36,"That's about as much as we have."],[401.2,"And it's a longish paragraph, so there's not a lot of way to sort of dissect that paragraph and figure out a bunch of details about who wrote it or exactly to whom or exactly what was going on."],[417.24,"The Epistle of St."],[418.36,"Jude is primarily sort of a criticism and warning and prophecy against false teachers, but even their false teaching isn't really sort of laid out in detail."],[431.24,"So and there are plenty of false teachers wandering around."],[435.08,"So we don't even know exactly what they were teaching."],[439.0,"On the other hand, that makes Saint Jude important, even though it is so short, is and interesting is the way that Saint Jude interacts with Old Testament and then other ancient literature, sort of adjacent to the Old Testament interacts with those stories and applies them to his present situation in a first century Christian community."],[465.36,"So that I think is the primary reason why it ends up being included in the New Testament."],[473.52,"You know, you may wonder that, like, well, why is and St."],[477.44,"Jude's epistle ends up in the New Testament relatively late because most people had never seen it."],[482.24,"Um, the first place where we see it is around Rome, which is more evidence that that's sort of where it's from."],[489.04,"Um shows up sort of relatively late, and if the whole text is basically in 2 Peter, then why is it in the Bible?"],[495.84,"I think that's why."],[497.44,"I think that's sort of the the importance of it."],[501.28,"But so all that said, by way of mini introduction, uh the Epistle of St."],[508.04,"Jude, unless there's any questions or comments or points of order or votes that need to be taken, we'll go ahead and pick up uh in verse one, because there's no chapters, of uh the Epistle of St."],[522.92,"Jude. Which begins Jude, a bond servant of Jesus Christ and brother of James."],[530.44,"So, as we mentioned, that's the only introduction we get."],[533.8,"Bondservant, of course, is basically the word for slave."],[537.24,"Um this is the, I guess."],[541.48,"New King James was new enough to be politically correct, I guess."],[545.92,"But that's literally what it means."],[550.16,"To those who are called sanctified by God the Father and preserved in Jesus Christ."],[557.52,"So you can see the similarity in that opening to the opening of St."],[563.6,"Paul's epistles. So even though this is a very short text, like we saw with Philemon that St."],[569.92,"Paul wrote, which is also very short, this is. a way similar to what we saw in 1 Peter and in St"],[582.2,"Paul's epistle of a of addressing the church."],[585.58,"Addressing the Christian community."],[589.78,"And incorporate both God the Father is the one who calls and sanctifies, meaning makes holy, sets apart."],[595.95,"As a people, makes them a community, makes them a people, and then that community and that people is preserved in Christ."],[605.71,"So verse 2, mercy, peace, and love be multiplied to you."],[610.75,"Beloved, while I was very diligent to write to you concerning our common salvation, I found it necessary to write to you, exhorting you to contend earnestly for the faith which was once for all delivered to the saints."],[624.79,"So he um, we saw uh St. says."],[628.15,"Paul do this in 1 Corinthians."],[630.23,"Um, he says, there's a one letter that I wanted to write to you, but I gotta write a different letter."],[636.05,"So I I wanted to write a letter to you that would just be sort of educational, edifying, talking about our salvation, encouraging you."],[645.43,"That kind of thing."],[646.79,"Uh but there's trouble in River City, so we've got something else we gotta talk about."],[654.07,"And specifically, um, he needs to exhort them to contend earnestly for the faith which was once and for all delivered to the saints."],[664.92,"So he's saying, originally."],[669.14,"Originally, there was this faith."],[672.11,"This gospel that was preached to you, that was deposited here, that made you a community."],[677.65,"That happened once and for, and it was supposed to be once and for all, right"],[681.37,"Meaning you were supposed to maintain that."],[683.37,"You were supposed to preserve that."],[685.98,"He says, Well, something's happened."],[688.8,"Things are slipping."],[690.08,"You're moving away from it."],[692.02,"And not only do you need to not move away from it, you need to fight for it."],[697.22,"You need to fight to protect it and defend it."],[701.14,"And so that's why I'm writing to you."],[703.14,"So this is his purpose statement right here."],[705.78,"You need to not only not let this slip, you need to be ready to fight back against those who are questioning it, those who are trying to draw you away from it, um, to protect it."],[719.94,"Verse 4, for cer.tain men have crept in unnoticed, who long ago were marked out for this condemnation"],[728.06,"Ungodly men who turn the grace of our God into lewdness and deny the only Lord God and our Lord Jesus Christ."],[737.5,"What is this condemnation?"],[738.78,"This condemnation is the condemnation he's now doing."],[743.54,"So this isn't that these guys showed up and they were nice guys, they were wonderful, and now they've drifted."],[749.24,"He's saying these guys started out bad."],[752.5,"They they they joined your community under false pretenses."],[758.92,"They're up to no good."],[760.56,"They're there for the wrong reason."],[762.8,"They've crept in."],[763.72,"in They snuck while you weren't looking."],[763.88,"And though we don't get a lot of detail here we get they turn the grace of our God into lewdness."],[774.69,"Meaning they're exchanging what God is wanting to do in the community."],[782.39,"The work of God in the community."],[784.63,"Buy them together and make them a community."],[787.91,"The love that God is crafting there."],[789.91,"Instead of that, they're after some kind of lewdness."],[794.2,"So they're in it for themselves, and this is particularly taking the form of sexual immorality."],[801.26,"Not that that ever there are ever problems with that in churches."],[805.52,"But that's um even in the first century, the same thing happens."],[812.8,"And by doing that, by doing that, this isn't just like, oh, they're breaking our moral code."],[819.69,"Like we're Christians, we have rules."],[822.67,"But by doing this, by rejecting what God is doing in the community, and instead trying to please themselves and their own appetites, they're denying God."],[834.56,"Because God's presence is trying to do this other thing."],[838.1,"So they're denying God, they're denying Christ."],[842.28,"Now notice the phrasing here."],[844.92,"The only Lord, God. and our Lord Jesus Christ"],[853.64,"You can't say only Lord and then name two."],[858.8,"So this is just another note of Trinitarianism already here in the right in the New Testament."],[871.6,"So verse five."],[874.8,"But I want to remind you, though you once knew this, that the Lord, having saved the people out of the land of Egypt, afterward destroyed those who did not believe."],[884.56,"This is the first verse I have to correct"],[887.12,"This one, though, sort of isn't the New King James Version's fault."],[891.2,"Um because at the time this was translated, they didn't know any better."],[896.52,"So about 30 years ago now."],[905.4,"Uh uh, we discovered uh a much older, actually, a couple of much older uh papyrus fragments, but even as a fragment, it has a pretty big chunk of St."],[920.36,"Jude's epistle because it's a paragraph."],[922.68,"Um of St."],[925.16,"Jude. And at least two of them included this verse, verse five, and they both had something different than what's here."],[936.5,"Namely, instead of saying though you want to that the Lord having saved the people out of the land of Egypt, it said that Jesus having saved the people out of the land of Egypt."],[950.34,"And one of the one of the rules, these are scholarly rules, so you can ignore them if you want to."],[958.34,"But when you're doing this sort of thing, and you have different copies of the text, of the same book of the New Testament, say, and they have different readings."],[969.6,"You're going, well, which which one of these should we go with?"],[973.21,"Which one of these is the the more important one?"],[978.01,"Sometimes people just say, well, whatever's oldest."],[981.62,"It's not always right."],[984.1,"But in this case, the oldest has Jesus."],[986.58,"But one of the one of the more important rules of thumb is that if you have one reading that seems weird or more difficult or harder to understand, it's more likely that that was sort of the the original reading."],[1008.82,"And then the one that's easier came later."],[1011.8,"Because if I'm a scribe and I'm confused."],[1014.7,"If I'm making a copy and I'm confused, or I'm making a copy and something doesn't make sense to me, I'm more likely to change it into something that's easy and makes easy sense than I am to take something that makes perfect sense and make it weird."],[1032.87,"So the reading of just the Lord saved people out of Egypt."],[1037.6,"That's you know, you buzz right past that."],[1040.62,"Whereas Jesus saved people out of Egypt and then destroyed them in the wilderness, you're kind of like, wait, what?"],[1047.79,"Um but uh I think the biggest reason why this is this is the more important reading is actually that what St."],[1060.67,"Jude is doing here, if he's talking about Jesus, matches what he's going to do with a bunch of other Old Testament and texts that are near the Old Testament stories that he's about to go through and how he's going to interpret them."],[1077.03,"Okay. So what would he mean then if he's talking about Jesus leading leading the people out of Egypt?"],[1086.15,"So the most likely thing he would be talking about is a very common identification in the story of the Exodus."],[1095.11,"So in the story of the Exodus, although it isn't read or talked about much and hasn't been in any of the movies, uh, there's a point where Moses is told uh by God that God is going to send this particular angel to lead them out of Egypt."],[1118.53,"Like before he does it."],[1120.61,"He's gonna lead them through the wilderness, lead them into the promised land, and help them conquer it."],[1125.99,"And he says, This this angel's not just any angel, because he has my name in him."],[1133.19,"And so he's gonna punish all of your sins."],[1135.67,"There's a whole there's a whole thing."],[1137.51,"And this angel is referred to as the angel of the Lord in the text."],[1141.03,"There's a whole bunch of places where he appears."],[1143.83,"He's actually with Israel from the Exodus all the way until the beginning of the book of Judges, which is another place people don't read often."],[1153.43,"The first chapter of the book of of Judges, he gets kind of fed up with Israel's wickedness and goes and stands in front them all and makes this speech about why he's leaving."],[1164.56,"Why he's he's departing."],[1167.04,"And this angel appears a bunch of places, as I mentioned."],[1170.48,"One of the key places that becomes really important in terms of Christian interpretation is we don't talk about that angel as much."],[1180.24,"We're maybe more familiar with at the Exodus as Israel's being led out of Egypt and through the wilderness, there's that pillar of fire at night and pillar of smoke during the day that kind of leads them."],[1194.0,"Well, when in Exodus, Israel comes up to the edge of the Sea of Reeds, and Pharaoh's army is coming up behind them, and they get worried, Moses is uh talking to God about what they're gonna do."],[1211.69,"And while he's talking to God, it says that the angel and the pillar go around behind Israel and put themselves between Israel and the Egyptian army."],[1226.27,"Like to protect them."],[1229.47,"And so this image of this voice of God speaking to Moses, the angel of the Lord, and this pillar of fire and smoke, thereby the water, gets picked up very early in Christian writings and connected to what happens at Christ's baptism at the water."],[1257.27,"Where you have the voice of the Father, you have Christ, and you have the Holy Spirit, appearing."],[1263.85,"Let's get connected as a manifestation of the Trinity."],[1268.49,"And there's a whole lot more to be said about the angel of the Lord being Christ of the Old Testament."],[1273.71,"But um, and I mean this this identification is happening, we know in the second century."],[1279.79,"As early as we have writings in the second century, they're making this connection."],[1283.79,"So it's not a huge stretch."],[1286.6,"That St. Jude is already right thinking about that, that connection."],[1293.93,"And so the point he's making here then is that the same Christ who brought you together and made you a community, like he was just talking about."],[1305.18,"That same Christ who brought you together and made you a community, brought Israel together and made it a nation in the Exodus."],[1314.35,"But then after that, when the people turned on God, when they started grumbling and complaining, saying, Hey, we should just kill Moses out here in the desert and go back to Egypt."],[1325.52,"When things went that way, they ended up dying in the desert."],[1328.96,"They ended up dying in the wilderness."],[1330.68,"They didn't make it to the promised land."],[1335.24,"And so St."],[1336.28,"Jude is holding out that same possibility to them."],[1339.56,"Because remember, you made this good start, you received this faith, this faith was delivered to you."],[1346.52,"But if you don't hold fast to it, if you don't protect it, if you don't defend it, you can end up you can end up just like them."],[1354.52,"And so it's important that that's Christ in the Old Testament."],[1359.56,"It's important that that's Jesus because you want to cut off the idea, which may be something the false teachers were saying, that like, oh yeah, that's the way things worked in the old covenant, maybe."],[1375.36,"But now. Now this is different."],[1379.07,"God loves us, Jesus loves us."],[1380.51,"We can do we can participate in our lewdness or whatever else, that we want to do, because now it's right, it's open season."],[1387.73,"And so say Jude making the point, like, no, that was the same Christ, is pertinent, to to the argument he's making."],[1396.15,"Yes, sir. Uh I thought you were probably gonna say that what was strange about this verse was believe."],[1403.19,"Destroy those who do not believe."],[1404.85,"Usually you say that."],[1405.94,"Which we could also translate who were not faithful."],[1408.34,"Okay. Because it's the same word."],[1410.18,"Same word. Yeah, the word is translated believe, or the word that's translated faith are the same word."],[1415.96,"And faith is more less the idea of ideas in your head, signing a doctrinal statement, taking a truefalse test, and more about loyalty, allegiance."],[1427.73,"Faithfulness in that sense."],[1434.29,"Verse 6. And the angels who did not keep their proper domain, but left their own abode, he has reserved an everlasting chains under darkness for the judgment of the great day."],[1450.13,"Okay. So, what example?"],[1453.41,"Israel started out great, didn't end up so great, in the wilderness."],[1460.07,"These angels didn't, you know, started out angels."],[1466.25,"But they didn't hold on to what they had."],[1469.78,"They went astray."],[1471.51,"And notice again, he still has this lewdness, in the background."],[1479.85,"So um the uh which direction to go with this?"],[1489.29,"I'll go this scripture."],[1490.73,"So there is, strictly speaking, no place in the Old Testament, or the new testament, but in the Old Testament that talks about any angels leaving their former estate."],[1503.61,"So that's one singular"],[1506.05,"Other than maybe the devil."],[1512.06,"So what story is he referring to?"],[1514.22,"Well, we get a clue in that reserved an everlasting chains under darkness for the judgment of the great day."],[1523.58,"Because that language is taken from the book of Enoch."],[1530.22,"And I'm not just picking the book of Enoch at random because this story is told in other places."],[1536.06,"We're going to see it a little bit."],[1537.58,"St. Jude's going to quote the book of Enoch."],[1540.94,"So he's going to mention Enoch specifically in that context."],[1545.18,"So pretty definitive proof he had that book in the back of his head."],[1551.16,"And he's using this specific language of the everlasting chains and under darkness that doesn't occur anywhere else."],[1557.97,"And in the book of Enoch, uh, the way in which those angelic beings fall is through some kind of concupiscence with human women."],[1570.93,"Now, exactly what that means is another question that we won't go into right now necessarily."],[1575.81,"When we loop back around after Revelation and get to Genesis 6, we'll talk about it there."],[1581.17,"But um, but so this idea of this lewdness, this profligacy, and that being a denial of God who you're supposed to be serving."],[1590.97,"That's what grace is"],[1592.5,"God created the angels so that he could do certain works through them."],[1597.78,"Right? That's remember his original setting out God's grace versus pursuing your own pleasure."],[1606.34,"So these angels are another example of that."],[1608.44,"So he's saying, look, this even happened to angels."],[1612.13,"This even happened to angels."],[1613.81,"It can happen to you."],[1618.93,"Verse 7 As Sodom and Gomorrah and the cities around them in a similar manner to these, having given themselves over to sexual immorality and gone after strange flesh."],[1633.49,"So if you if you weren't convinced that he's talking about this story in the book of Enoch, notice this that the the um the people of Sodom and Gomorrah in the same way as the angels he's talking about went after strange flesh."],[1657.11,"Remember, the two angels went into Sodom to bring Lot and his family out, and the men of the city attempted to rape them."],[1668.21,"So he's paralleling, he says, in the same way, the language of strange flesh."],[1673.75,"It's pretty clear what he's talking about when he talks about the angels."],[1678.83,"They're set forth as an example, suffering the vengeance of eternal fire."],[1686.11,"The judgment, uh, like I said, the judgment of the last day."],[1689.09,"So all these are examples."],[1690.7,"This can happen to you, if you if you neglect what was entrusted to you."],[1697.52,"Verse 8, likewise also these dreamers defile the flesh, reject authority, and speak evil of dignitaries."],[1707.36,"Dignitaries. Um so it's literally dominions."],[1713.3,"Like the rank of angels."],[1715.2,"Rank of angels."],[1717.84,"So again, we don't know exactly, what they're doing, but it involves clearly involves some kind of lewdness."],[1728.55,"The defiling of the flesh, some kind of sexual immorality is involved."],[1732.15,"Um rejecting authority presumably refers to the church authorities."],[1738.17,"They're not following the church authorities, they're setting themselves up as their own authority, and they're in some way speaking evil or evilly of the heavenly powers."],[1752.19,"Which could just be some kind of false teaching."],[1755.05,"Like if you think about um early forms of Gnosticism that have a sort of bizarre array of emanations and beingings and that kind of th."],[1764.57,"Um that they're involved in sort of semi-worship activity"],[1770.77,"It could be something related to that, but we aren't given enough detail to know exactly."],[1778.53,"Verse 9 yet Michael the Archangel, in contending with the devil, when he disputed about the body of Moses, dare not bring against him a reviling accusation, but said, The Lord rebuke you."],[1795.17,"So he's offering this as a counterexample."],[1799.3,"So if you want, you know, the worst guy ever, that's the devil."],[1803.34,"So when the Archangel Michael is contending with him, he didn't curse at him, slander him."],[1811.08,"He said, the Lord rebuke you."],[1813.54,"So, i.e. St."],[1816.98,"Michael even had some kind of respect for the devil."],[1821.16,"Whereas these people have no respect for anyone, is the idea."],[1824.76,"But now notice he refers to this time when St."],[1828.28,"Michael the Archangel fought with the devil over Moses' body."],[1833.16,"Which again, you're not going to find in the Old Testament."],[1836.41,"So this is this is one of the books."],[1840.34,"We talked about this already as we're going through St."],[1842.42,"Paul. St. Paul refers to things that aren't actually in the Old Testament, that are in these other texts all the time."],[1848.36,"And we talked about there, that's worth repeating here because St."],[1849.8,"This isn't St"],[1851.76,"Jude's already done it, and he's going to do more of it."],[1855.73,"Jude saying, hey, this thing I'm referring to really ought to be in the Old Testament."],[1861.51,"He doesn't have the idea of a canon of the Old Testament in his head."],[1866.79,"That's not an idea that existed in the first century."],[1870.45,"Is this from Jews?"],[1873.01,"No, it's from another."],[1875.97,"It's from the um Assumption of Moses."],[1880.57,"Um, we've got two different versions of that."],[1883.81,"So we're not sure which exactly he's referring to."],[1886.45,"So it's sort of like what St."],[1887.57,"Paul referred to Janice and Jambris."],[1889.89,"We've got all these texts where that's the name of Pharaoh's wizards, so we don't know which one he's referring to in particular, if he's referring to one in particular, and not just using the names."],[1900.01,"But so what this is is that everyone in the first century who's practicing some form of Judaism has received a set of sort of authoritative texts."],[1912.81,"At least the Torah, and then probably some more stuff."],[1916.57,"The exact list is different."],[1919.06,"But they've also received it as part of this whole tradition."],[1924.66,"They've received it as part of this whole way of life that's been handed down to them."],[1928.88,"Of their daily cycle of prayer, the way they worship."],[1932.5,"The way they worship at home, the way they worship when they gather in a synagogue, the way they worship, when they go to the temple, the way things have been interpreted traditionally down through the years, the relationship that's been understood between different texts, other texts that are sort of adjacent to the ones they think are authoritative, but that they think shed light on those."],[1954.88,"And that's all just one big thing together."],[1958.32,"And they quote it all the same way."],[1961.92,"So St. Paul, when he talks about the rock following Israel in the wilderness, he doesn't say, now I know that's not actually in the Torah, but uh I have it on good authority from a couple of rabbis who taught me that that's how it worked."],[1976.61,"He just cites it because it's all part of the same thing."],[1980.9,"It's all part of the same thing."],[1982.34,"They weren't just like handed a Hebrew Bible and told, Oh, everything you need to know is in there."],[1989.62,"Just read it for yourself and you know, come to your own conclusions."],[1993.24,"That's not how Judaism ever worked, and that's not how Christianity ever worked before the 16th century."],[2002.28,"Um, and this is another example."],[2007.42,"So there's at the end of Deuteronomy, there's this sort of reference that like God buries Moses or something, like Moses' body just kind of disappears, and nobody knows where it is or what happened to it to this very day."],[2023.0,"And that's kind of it."],[2025.54,"But what we get in in these Assumption of Moses stories are the stories of how his body was taken up into heaven."],[2034.74,"So Moses gets sort of resurrected early."],[2038.1,"It's not like Enoch and Elijah who don't die."],[2041.79,"And go, do not pass go."],[2044.13,"Do not collect $200, just go straight to the Heavenly Council."],[2048.61,"But his body is taken up and he's sort of resurrected already."],[2054.27,"So those traditions have already kind of been endorsed in the New Testament before we get here to this reference when the Mount of Transfiguration was described."],[2067.67,"So remember, Christ is standing there talking to Elijah and Moses."],[2079.59,"Moses does too."],[2081.93,"It's not like the ghost of Moses or Moses' soul floating there as a sphere or something."],[2087.85,"Sorry, origin. But uh so he's already sort of resurrected."],[2096.35,"And the idea here in the story, the reason why we have this sort of fight between the devil and St."],[2103.31,"Michael, is the idea that so we have to remember we've all got Plato brain."],[2111.42,"So we think your soul is a sphere or something, and it lives inside your body, and that's the real you."],[2117.28,"You're kind of wearing a body."],[2118.88,"That's not how anyone in the Jewish world looked at it."],[2124.48,"So in the Jewish world, you are an animated body."],[2129.92,"Animated coming from the Latin word for soul."],[2133.17,"Anima. So when you the reason you die, the reason that's death, is that your soul, your life, is pulled out of your body."],[2144.19,"The life leaves your body."],[2147.07,"And since your body has no life in it, it is subject to decay."],[2152.21,"But sin was not seen then to be just a matter of your soul."],[2158.77,"Like sin somehow leaves some kind of stain on your soul or something."],[2163.23,"Sin affects you, body and soul, both."],[2168.68,"And so sin is what is responsible for the corruption of your body."],[2174.12,"It's what makes your body mortal in the first place."],[2177.1,"But it's responsible for the corruption of your body."],[2181.98,"And so when you see depictions, all the way back in Genesis 3, when the devil gets thrown out of heaven."],[2189.76,"It says he's gonna crawl at his belly, he's gonna eat dust."],[2194.02,"So ancient people weren't dumb."],[2196.34,"They didn't think snakes ate dirt."],[2198.58,"They had seen them eat mice."],[2201.27,"They'd seen it too."],[2202.71,"Okay. Um the the the dust, the word for dust there is the same word that's used for Adam's composition."],[2213.99,"Dust you are to dust you will return."],[2216.87,"So this is where the whole iconography we see of that thing that looks like a big serpent swallowing people, like that big gaping maw of Hades."],[2226.0,"Death. That's where that comes from."],[2229.2,"That idea. So the idea here is Moses dies, and so the devil comes to claim his body."],[2236.96,"Back to dust"],[2238.18,"To come and corrupt it, destroy it."],[2242.78,"But because Moses is righteous, St."],[2244.62,"Michael shows up to defend him."],[2247.68,"Takes his body up to heaven and he's resurrected there."],[2252.24,"So on August 15th, when we celebrate the dormition of the Theotokos, and her body disappears from the tomb."],[2261.16,"We're not setting her up as parallel to Christ and his ascension."],[2266.6,"We're setting her up as parallel to Moses."],[2271.98,"That because she's righteous and because she's holy, corruption can't claim her body."],[2279.52,"And so it's taken up to heaven and she's resurrected early, the way Moses was."],[2287.44,"This is also where we get the tradition in the church of saints who have bodies that are incorrupt."],[2294.9,"Where certain saints, certain holy people, when they die, their bodies don't decompose the way."],[2301.22,"Mine's gonna stink, I assure you, but uh theirs does not necessarily, in certain cases."],[2307.6,"So this is one of the ways God uses to reveal to us that someone was a saint, was a holy person."],[2314.17,"Because we're sinful, we don't always recognize that in people."],[2317.95,"But this is one of the ways God can reveal it to us."],[2321.55,"So that's the idea that's going on behind those stories."],[2325.73,"But so this story is so commonly accepted as just being what happened when Moses died that St."],[2333.41,"Jude just refers to it offhand."],[2335.73,"Doesn't have to qualify it."],[2337.09,"Everybody knows what he's talking about."],[2340.5,"Because it's part of the tradition."],[2342.36,"That's been received."],[2343.32,"It doesn't matter if it showed up in somebody's canon later or not."],[2347.24,"Um and he could use it as an example then of how even Saint Michael even showed respect for the devil."],[2354.89,"And therefore, how can you act in this way that's disrespectful, rejecting of authority?"],[2364.18,"Verse 10, but these, the people he's talking about, these false teachers, speak evil of whatever they do not know."],[2372.98,"And whatever they know naturally, like brute beasts, and these things they corrupt themselves."],[2380.39,"So they're all these things they don't understand."],[2384.17,"Those things they don't understand, they feel free to talk about."],[2388.19,"And end up issuing all kinds of blasphemy and slander and everything because they don't know what they're talking about."],[2393.71,"And then the things they do know about, they behave corruptly."],[2399.18,"They know about their earthly desires and they just corrupt themselves with them."],[2404.46,"Verse 11: Woe to them, for they have gone in the way of Cain, have run greedily in the error of Balaam for prophet, and perished in the rebellion of Korah."],[2415.86,"So he's just, these are all three Old Testament examples, but he's just throwing them out."],[2420.16,"He's throwing them out one after another."],[2422.16,"So Cain, obviously, killed his brother."],[2426.9,"But Cain also became by this point in the Jewish mind the archetypal sinner."],[2434.44,"Who not only sinned but, like taught his descendants to sin."],[2438.61,"And so if you read like Josephus, who's also writing in the first century about Cain, he has Cain as like the first heretic."],[2448.39,"That he was. he was actively like starting his own religion, that actively like starting all this"],[2453.31,"So Cain is sort of this archetypal figure by this point, who he's comparing them to."],[2461.39,"They've run greeling error of Balaam."],[2463.39,"Remember, Balaam took money to curse the people of God."],[2468.32,"And perished in the rebellion of Korah."],[2470.64,"So Korah and his sons rebelled against uh Moses and Aaron."],[2476.32,"They came and basically accused Moses and Aaron of not having been appointed by God as leaders."],[2482.32,"Aaron in particular is high priest, because they were from one of the priestly families, of having appointed himself, basically, and lording it over them, and said, We should be, you know, there should be a high priest."],[2492.96,"You can decide for yourself if that sounds like anybody else"],[2493.48,"We should all be equal."],[2498.92,"But uh should be high."],[2502.52,"We should all be, you've just put yourself over us."],[2504.92,"God didn't say anything to you, and uh they ended up having the ground open up and swallow them."],[2510.6,"Um so these are the kind of people who these folks are being compared to by St."],[2517.08,"Chute. So I think you can get it, even though we don't have the specifics of what they taught, you get an idea."],[2522.92,"Not good folks."],[2526.52,"Listen next time as Father Stephen De Young continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2533.88,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecouncil at ancient faith dot com."],[2537.0,"at ancient faith. That's wholecouncil.com"]],"o":[{"t":23.52,"lv":2,"h":"Intro: why Jude comes first","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Epistle of <b>St. Jude</b> - short, <b>no chapter numbers</b>, just verses</li><li class=\"d1\">all one chapter; in Greek a \"longish paragraph\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Doing Jude <b>out of order</b> - between 1 and 2 Peter, not after 1-2-3 John</li><li class=\"d1\">almost whole text of Jude is <b>incorporated into 2 Peter 2</b> - \"one of the weird things in the New Testament\"</li><li class=\"d1\">so Jude clearly written <b>first</b>, then folded into 2 Peter</li><li class=\"d0\">Talk about Jude now; <b>2 Peter</b> when we next get together - \"a little while\"</li><li class=\"d1\">more on the link when we reach <b>2 Peter 2</b></li></ul>"},{"t":118.34,"lv":2,"h":"Author: Jude, brother of James","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Identifies himself as <b>Jude, brother of James</b> (v.1)</li><li class=\"d1\">\"vaguest sort of intro\" in Palestinian Judaism - <b>Judah, brother of Jacob</b> - \"probably like a thousand of those\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Traditionally = one of the <b>brothers of the Lord</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Lord's sons/brothers listed include one named <b>Judas</b> or Jude</li><li class=\"d1\"><i>Judas</i> = Greek form of <b>Judah</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>James</b> the brother of the Lord = major figure in early church (intro to James)</li><li class=\"d0\">Some \"spasmodically\" doubt Jude wrote it - <b>no real reason to</b></li></ul>"},{"t":197.75,"lv":3,"h":"Why doubting authorship is weak","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>No apocryphal texts</b> attributed to this St. Jude</li><li class=\"d1\">not a famous figure whose name someone would borrow</li><li class=\"d1\">even if a raft of pseudepigrapha existed and were all lost - <b>intro doesn't say</b> \"brother of the Lord\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Faking authority is done <b>less casually</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Abe Lincoln test: not \"a book by Abraham from Illinois\" but \"Abraham Lincoln, <b>16th president</b> of the United States...\"</li><li class=\"d1\">real pseudepigrapha open with a <b>big paragraph</b> of credentials</li><li class=\"d1\">apocryphal <b>James</b> literature: \"first bishop of Jerusalem, initiated into the secret mysteries by Christ himself after his resurrection...\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Jude: nothing else claims his name → weird to claim falsely</li><li class=\"d0\">Simplest: <b>yeah, he probably wrote it</b> - otherwise \"unremarkable\", left no other literature</li></ul>"},{"t":354.12,"lv":2,"h":"Where from: Rome","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Connection to 2 Peter → most plausible origin = <b>Christian community in Rome</b> (the city itself)</li><li class=\"d1\">2 Peter clearly from there</li><li class=\"d1\">2 Peter incorporates <b>traditional Roman teaching and preaching</b> from St. Peter and others - Jude gets incorporated</li><li class=\"d0\">Background = about all we have</li><li class=\"d1\">a longish paragraph - can't dissect it for who exactly, to whom, what was going on</li></ul>"},{"t":417.24,"lv":3,"h":"Content and why it's in the NT","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Jude = <b>criticism, warning, prophecy against false teachers</b></li><li class=\"d1\">their false teaching <b>not laid out in detail</b> - \"plenty of false teachers wandering around\" - don't know what they taught</li><li class=\"d0\">What makes it <b>important</b> though short: how Jude uses the <b>Old Testament</b> and <b>adjacent ancient literature</b></li><li class=\"d1\">applies those stories to a <b>first-century Christian community</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"primary reason\" it ends up in the New Testament</li><li class=\"d0\">Entered NT <b>relatively late</b> - most had never seen it</li><li class=\"d1\">first seen <b>around Rome</b> → more evidence for origin</li><li class=\"d1\">if whole text is in 2 Peter, why in the Bible? - <b>that's why</b></li></ul>"},{"t":522.92,"lv":2,"h":"Jude 1-2 - Greeting","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.1</b>: \"Jude, a bond servant of Jesus Christ and brother of James\"</li><li class=\"d1\">only intro we get</li><li class=\"d1\">bondservant = basically word for <b>slave</b> - \"New King James was new enough to be politically correct\"</li><li class=\"d0\">To those <b>called, sanctified by God the Father, preserved in Jesus Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">similar to opening of <b>St. Paul's epistles</b></li><li class=\"d1\">short like <b>Philemon</b>; addresses the church, the Christian <b>community</b> as in <b>1 Peter</b> and Paul</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>God the Father</b> calls and sanctifies = makes holy, <b>sets apart</b></li><li class=\"d1\">makes them a <b>people</b>, a community</li><li class=\"d1\">community then <b>preserved in Christ</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.2</b>: mercy, peace and love be multiplied</li></ul>"},{"t":605.71,"lv":2,"h":"Jude 3 - Contend for the faith","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.3</b>: wanted to write on \"our common salvation\" - found it necessary to exhort them</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul does this in <b>1 Corinthians</b> - one letter I wanted to write, another I have to</li><li class=\"d1\">wanted: educational, edifying, encouraging</li><li class=\"d1\">\"but there's <b>trouble in River City</b>\" - something else to talk about</li><li class=\"d0\">Exhort to <b>contend earnestly</b> for the faith <b>once for all delivered</b> to the saints</li><li class=\"d1\">the gospel preached, <b>deposited</b> with you, made you a community</li><li class=\"d1\">once and for all = you were to <b>maintain</b> and <b>preserve</b> it</li><li class=\"d1\">now: things <b>slipping</b>, moving away</li><li class=\"d1\">not only don't move away - <b>fight for it</b>, protect and defend</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Purpose statement</b> of the letter</li><li class=\"d1\">ready to <b>fight back</b> against those questioning it or drawing you away</li></ul>"},{"t":719.94,"lv":2,"h":"Jude 4 - Men crept in","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.4</b>: certain men <b>crept in unnoticed</b>, long ago marked out for this <b>condemnation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">condemnation = the one he's now doing</li><li class=\"d1\">not nice guys who drifted - <b>started bad</b>, joined under <b>false pretenses</b>, \"snuck in while you weren't looking\"</li><li class=\"d0\">They turn the <b>grace of God into lewdness</b></li><li class=\"d1\">exchanging what God wants to do in the community: <b>bind together</b>, the <b>love</b> God is crafting</li><li class=\"d1\">instead: lewdness - in it <b>for themselves</b>, esp. <b>sexual immorality</b> - same in the 1st century</li><li class=\"d0\">Not just breaking \"our moral code\"</li><li class=\"d1\">rejecting what God does in community + pleasing appetites = <b>denying God</b>, denying Christ</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"The only Lord God and our Lord Jesus Christ\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">can't say \"only Lord\" and name two → <b>Trinitarianism</b> already in the NT</li></ul>"},{"t":871.6,"lv":2,"h":"Jude 5 - Lord (Jesus) saved from Egypt","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.5</b>: Lord, <b>having saved the people out of Egypt</b>, afterward <b>destroyed those who did not believe</b></li><li class=\"d1\">first verse he has to <b>correct</b> - not NKJV's fault; at the time they didn't know better</li><li class=\"d0\">~<b>30 years ago</b>: older <b>papyrus fragments</b> found, big chunk of Jude</li><li class=\"d1\">at least two read <b>\"Jesus\"</b> having saved the people out of Egypt, not \"the Lord\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Textual rules of thumb (scholarly - \"ignore if you want\")</li><li class=\"d1\">oldest isn't always right - but here oldest has <b>Jesus</b></li><li class=\"d1\">the <b>weirder / harder</b> reading likely original; easier came later</li><li class=\"d1\">scribe more likely to <b>simplify</b> than make sense weird</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Lord saved them\" - buzz right past; \"<b>Jesus</b> saved them then destroyed them in the wilderness\" - \"wait, what?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Biggest reason: matches what Jude does with the <b>other OT stories</b> he's about to interpret</li></ul>"},{"t":1077.03,"lv":3,"h":"The Angel of the Lord","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Jesus leading out of Egypt = common identification in <b>Exodus</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God tells Moses he'll send an <b>angel</b> to lead them out, through wilderness, into the land, help conquer it</li><li class=\"d1\">\"not just any angel - <b>has my name in him</b>\" - punishes sins</li><li class=\"d1\">called the <b>angel of the Lord</b> - appears many places, with Israel from <b>Exodus to start of Judges</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Judges 1: fed up with wickedness, stands before them, speech on why he's <b>leaving</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Sea of Reeds</b>: pharaoh's army behind</li><li class=\"d1\">angel and <b>pillar of fire/cloud</b> go behind Israel, <b>between</b> them and Egyptians</li><li class=\"d0\">Voice of God to Moses + angel + pillar + the <b>water</b> → early Christian writers connect to <b>Christ's baptism</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Father's voice, Christ, <b>Holy Spirit</b> = manifestation of the <b>Trinity</b></li><li class=\"d1\">angel of the Lord = <b>Christ in the OT</b> - made in the <b>second century</b>, \"not a huge stretch\" Jude already thinking it</li></ul>"},{"t":1293.93,"lv":3,"h":"The point for Jude's readers","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Same Christ who <b>brought you together as a community</b> brought <b>Israel together as a nation</b> in the Exodus</li><li class=\"d1\">then people <b>turned on God</b>: grumbling, \"kill Moses and go back to Egypt\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>died in the desert</b>, never reached the promised land</li><li class=\"d0\">Jude holds out <b>same possibility</b> to them</li><li class=\"d1\">you made a good start, faith delivered to you</li><li class=\"d1\">don't hold fast, protect, defend → <b>end up like them</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Why \"it's <b>Jesus</b>\" matters</li><li class=\"d1\">cuts off false teachers' idea: \"that's how the <b>old covenant</b> worked, but now God loves us → <b>open season</b> on lewdness\"</li><li class=\"d1\">no: <b>same Christ</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1396.15,"lv":3,"h":"Q: believe vs. faithful","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: odd word - \"destroyed those who did not <b>believe</b>\"?</li><li class=\"d1\">same Greek word as <b>faith</b> - could be \"who were <b>not faithful</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Faith is not ideas in your head, <b>signing a doctrinal statement</b>, taking a true/false test</li><li class=\"d1\">more about <b>loyalty, allegiance</b>, faithfulness</li></ul>"},{"t":1434.29,"lv":2,"h":"Jude 6 - Angels who left","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.6</b>: angels who <b>did not keep their proper domain</b>, left their abode - <b>everlasting chains under darkness</b> for the judgment of the great day</li><li class=\"d1\">Israel started great, didn't end great; angels started as angels, <b>didn't hold on</b>, went astray</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>lewdness</b> still in the background</li><li class=\"d0\">No place in the OT (or NT) about angels leaving their former estate - \"other than maybe the devil\"</li><li class=\"d1\">clue: <b>\"everlasting chains under darkness\"</b> = language from the <b>book of Enoch</b> - occurs nowhere else</li><li class=\"d1\">Jude will <b>quote Enoch by name</b> shortly → \"pretty definitive proof\" it's in his head</li><li class=\"d0\">In <b>Enoch</b>: angels fall through <b>concupiscence with human women</b></li><li class=\"d1\">what that means: later - when we loop back after Revelation to <b>Genesis 6</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Lewdness = <b>denial of God</b> you're to serve</li><li class=\"d1\">God made angels for <b>works through them</b> - <b>grace vs. pursuing your pleasure</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"even this happened to <b>angels</b> - can happen to you\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1618.93,"lv":2,"h":"Jude 7 - Sodom and Gomorrah","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.7</b>: Sodom, Gomorrah and the cities around, <b>in a similar manner</b>, sexual immorality, <b>strange flesh</b></li><li class=\"d1\">confirms the <b>Enoch</b> story - \"in the same way as the angels\"</li><li class=\"d1\">two angels went to Sodom to bring out <b>Lot</b>; men tried to <b>rape</b> them</li><li class=\"d1\">parallel: \"strange flesh\" - clear what he means by the angels</li><li class=\"d0\">Set forth as an <b>example</b>, suffering <b>eternal fire</b></li><li class=\"d1\">= judgment of the <b>last day</b></li><li class=\"d0\">All examples: can happen to you <b>if you neglect what was entrusted</b> to you</li></ul>"},{"t":1697.52,"lv":2,"h":"Jude 8 - Dreamers","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.8</b>: these <b>dreamers</b> defile the flesh, <b>reject authority</b>, speak evil of <b>dignitaries</b></li><li class=\"d1\">dignitaries = literally <b>dominions</b> - <b>rank of angels</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Don't know exactly what they're doing, but</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>defiling the flesh</b> - some sexual immorality</li><li class=\"d1\">rejecting authority = presumably <b>church authorities</b> - set themselves up as their own authority</li><li class=\"d1\">speaking evil of <b>heavenly powers</b> - maybe false teaching</li><li class=\"d1\">like early <b>Gnosticism</b>: bizarre array of <b>emanations and beings</b>, semi-worship activity</li><li class=\"d1\">not enough detail to know</li></ul>"},{"t":1778.53,"lv":2,"h":"Jude 9 - Michael and the devil","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.9</b>: <b>Michael the Archangel</b> contending with the devil over the <b>body of Moses</b> - dared not bring a reviling accusation: \"<b>The Lord rebuke you</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">counterexample: the <b>worst guy ever</b> = devil - Michael didn't curse or slander</li><li class=\"d1\">even Michael had some respect → these have <b>none for anyone</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not in the Old Testament</li><li class=\"d1\">like Paul citing things not in the OT, all the time</li><li class=\"d1\">Jude: \"this really ought to be in the OT\" - <b>no idea of a canon</b> in the 1st century</li><li class=\"d0\">Source: the <b>Assumption of Moses</b></li><li class=\"d1\">two versions - unsure which he means</li><li class=\"d1\">like Paul on <b>Jannes and Jambres</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1900.01,"lv":3,"h":"Received tradition, not a canon","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Everyone practicing Judaism had received <b>authoritative texts</b> - at least <b>Torah</b>, probably more - list differed</li><li class=\"d1\">received as <b>whole tradition</b>: daily <b>prayer cycle</b>, worship at home, synagogue, temple</li><li class=\"d1\">how texts were interpreted, relations between texts, <b>adjacent texts</b> that shed light</li><li class=\"d1\">\"all just <b>one big thing</b>\" - quoted all the same way</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul on the <b>rock following Israel</b> - doesn't say \"not in the Torah, but a couple of rabbis told me\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not handed a Hebrew Bible: \"everything you need is in there, <b>come to your own conclusions</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">never how Judaism worked - nor Christianity <b>before the 16th century</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2002.28,"lv":3,"h":"Moses' body: body and soul","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">End of <b>Deuteronomy</b>: God buries Moses, body disappears, no one knows where</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Assumption of Moses</b> stories: body <b>taken up to heaven</b> - \"resurrected early\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not like <b>Enoch and Elijah</b> (no death - \"do not pass go, do not collect $200\")</li><li class=\"d1\">already endorsed in NT: <b>Transfiguration</b> - Christ with Elijah and Moses - not Moses' ghost or soul-sphere</li><li class=\"d0\">\"We've all got <b>Plato brain</b>\" - soul as sphere living in body, the real you</li><li class=\"d1\">Jewish view: you are an <b>animated body</b> - <i>anima</i> = Latin for soul</li><li class=\"d1\">death = <b>life pulled out</b> → body subject to <b>decay</b></li><li class=\"d1\">sin affects <b>body and soul</b> - responsible for <b>corruption</b> of the body, makes it mortal</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Genesis 3</b>: serpent crawls on belly, eats <b>dust</b></li><li class=\"d1\">ancient people knew snakes eat mice - <b>dust</b> = same word as Adam's composition: \"dust you are, to dust you return\"</li><li class=\"d1\">source of iconography: serpent-like <b>gaping maw of Hades</b>, Death</li><li class=\"d0\">So: Moses dies, <b>devil comes to claim body</b> → corrupt, back to dust</li><li class=\"d1\">Moses righteous → <b>Michael defends</b>, takes body to heaven, resurrected</li></ul>"},{"t":2229.2,"lv":3,"h":"Dormition and incorrupt saints","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>August 15</b>, <b>Dormition of the Theotokos</b> - body disappears from tomb</li><li class=\"d1\">not parallel to Christ's ascension - parallel to <b>Moses</b></li><li class=\"d1\">righteous and holy → <b>corruption can't claim her body</b>; taken up, resurrected early</li><li class=\"d0\">Origin also of <b>incorrupt saints</b>' bodies</li><li class=\"d1\">\"mine's gonna stink, I assure you\" - theirs not necessarily</li><li class=\"d1\">one way <b>God reveals</b> someone was a saint - \"because we're sinful, we don't always recognize it\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Story so <b>commonly accepted</b> that Jude refers to it offhand</li><li class=\"d1\">no need to qualify - <b>part of the received tradition</b>; doesn't matter if in someone's canon later</li><li class=\"d1\">example: even Michael showed respect for the devil → how can you reject authority?</li></ul>"},{"t":2364.18,"lv":2,"h":"Jude 10 - Brute beasts","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.10</b>: these <b>speak evil of whatever they do not know</b></li><li class=\"d1\">things they don't understand they feel free to talk about → <b>blasphemy and slander</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"don't know what they're talking about\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>What they know naturally</b>, like <b>brute beasts</b> - they <b>corrupt themselves</b></li><li class=\"d1\">earthly desires - they know them and corrupt themselves with them</li></ul>"},{"t":2404.46,"lv":2,"h":"Jude 11 - Cain, Balaam, Korah","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v.11</b>: <b>Woe to them</b> - three OT examples thrown out one after another</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Way of Cain</b></li><li class=\"d1\">killed his brother</li><li class=\"d1\">by now the <b>archetypal sinner</b> in Jewish mind - taught descendants to sin</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Josephus</b>: Cain as the <b>first heretic</b>, starting his own religion</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Error of Balaam</b></li><li class=\"d1\">took <b>money to curse</b> the people of God</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Rebellion of Korah</b></li><li class=\"d1\">with sons rebelled against <b>Moses and Aaron</b> - said not appointed by God, Aaron appointed himself high priest and lords it over them</li><li class=\"d1\">\"we should all be equal\" - \"you can decide for yourself if that sounds like anybody else\"</li><li class=\"d1\">ground <b>opened and swallowed</b> them</li><li class=\"d0\">Even without specifics of what they taught, \"you get an idea\" - <b>\"not good folks\"</b></li></ul>"}]});
